I had the following problem:

> I am just trying to switch my AlphaPC 164 SX running DU 4.0E from
> 10 Mbit (a 10Mbit TP/AUI/BNC DEC Tulip DE 435 with a DC 21040 on it) to
> 100Mbit.
> 
> I found a cheap board with a DC 21143 on it (it is a transtec T99FDEC),
> the SRM console recognizes it as ewb0 (when plugged in additionally to the
> DE 435 which is ewa0), "sho dev" shows its hardware address.
> 
> When  I replace the DE 435 with the T99FDEC (same PCI slot, so no kernel
> rebuild should be needed) it is recognized as ewa0, "set ewa0_mode
> FastFD" succeeds. After booting my kernel it finds a "tu0: Tulip", but
> then it says "hardware address: checksum error". Ok, the hardware address
> is not a DEC hardware adress "08:00:2b..." but something like
> "00:00:10:...", could this be the reason? 
> 
> My question: Has anyone such a "third party" Tulip card running, may be
> with a newer version (Tru64 4.0F or 4.0G), i.e. would an upgrade help me
> in this case?
> 

within 15 minutes I had three answers, which can be summed up as follows:

3rd party DC 21143 cards have to follow some design criteria in their PHY
media layer and SROM chip, my card doesn't. Upgrade would not help (I'll
check it anyway).

I have two choices: Ugrade to 4.0g (or 4.0f + patches) to get a Intel
EtherExpress PRO 10/100 working or to by a digital Networks DE 500
(Compaq DE 600?).

Just for the records: the manufacturer of the third party Tulip card
mentioned in my question did NOT claim that this card would be Tru64
compliant, so I'm not very surprised that it doesn't work. I just wanted
to check it myself, now I'll plug this card in one of my x86 Linux boxes.

Thanks to Kurt Ludwig, Thomas Leitner and Dr. Thomas Blinn for their quick
replies.

