Thanks to Kat,Wayne, Kamal, Devin for the help.  
 
All pointed me to this solution. 
 

delete the file 
 
/etc/.AM_is_running
 
and you should be OK...

And from your friendly command line is our friend guru  
                                        John Tan



Yes, I think that is right ... dxaccounts will not work when another user is
using it.  You can get on as root, use ps -aux to find the process using it,
kill that process, and then the GUI interface will work.  I don't know if
there is a lock file associated with dxaccounts; if there is, you might have
to delete an empty file to call up the GUI interface.
 
Otherwise, you should be able to use commands like useradd or adduser from
the command line interface.
 
Alternatively, use the traditional way of editing /etc/passwd, /etc/groups,
creating the HOME directory of the user, and using the passwd command to set
the user's password.  This should always work.  :-)
 
