The answer:

Limit the defragment to a single thread using the "-N 1" switch.  This 
mimics the behavior of a 4.x system. Without limiting the threads, 5.x 
defragment initiates a thread per fileset on the domain being 
fragmented.  For me this meant 3 threads running with a severe 
performance penalty.

Thanks to :

barry.carlson@compaq.com  
John P Speno <speno@isc.upenn.edu>

The original question:

I switched the hardware and OS at the same time on my primary 
mailserver.  We upgraded from a 4100 with 4 X 600 MHZ processors 
running 4.0F to an ES40 with 4  X 667 MHZ running 5.1 + PK 1.

This system supports IMAP mail via CYRUS for about 20,000 users and 
because the Cyrus IMAP is a file per mail message we have defragmented 
rather agressively.  on the old platform we ran defragmentation almost 
continuously with only minimal performance degradation noticeable to 
the users.  With the switch to the the new platform, defragmentation 
horribly degrades the IMAP performance.

I have limited the time defrag. runs to midnight to start of business 
day, but I am still getting complaints.

I have not yet tried "nicing" the defrags, but this is my next step.

Is there something fundamentally different in the 5.x ADVFS system in 
regards to defragmentation??

Any other suggestions for controlling this?



